      "type": "summary",
      "text": "## Executive summary\n\n**Markets barely differ on the share of loss-making order lines: from 18.6% (LATAM) to 18.9% (Pacific Asia), so Market is not the lever.**\n\nBy total profit, Europe contributes the most ($1.17M, 29.5% of the total) and Africa the least ($252K, 6.3%).\n\nBy total sales, Europe contributes the most ($10.87M, 29.6% of the total) and Africa the least ($2.29M, 6.2%).\n\nNo other branch passes the rule either: no segment with enough volume is more than 5 pp worse than the baseline of 18.7%, so this is a company-wide issue rather than a problem in one pocket.\n\n**Key drivers**\n- None passed the rule; the share of loss-making order lines is spread evenly across the business.\n\n**Recommendations**\n1. **Set a price floor so no order line is sold below cost.** Impact: High, effort: Medium.\n2. **Run a product-level profitability review.** Impact: High, effort: Medium.\n3. **Track the loss-making share monthly by market and category.** Impact: Medium, effort: Low.\n\n## Question\n\nWhere are we losing money, and is it a market problem? Measured by the share of loss-making order lines: Share of order lines that lose money (profit below zero), 0 to 1.\n\n## Issue tree and findings\n\n| Branch | Hypothesis | Verdict | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Logistics: Shipping Mode | Shipping costs make some modes unprofitable. | Ruled out | Every reliable segment is within 5 pp of 18.7%; worst is Standard Class at 18.9% (+0.1 pp). |\n| Geography: Market | Some markets sell at a loss more often (pricing, discounts, costs). | Ruled out | Every reliable segment is within 5 pp of 18.7%; worst is Pacific Asia at 18.9% (+0.2 pp). |\n| Customer: Customer Segment | Some customer types get unprofitable deals. | Ruled out | Every reliable segment is within 5 pp of 18.7%; worst is Home Office at 18.9% (+0.1 pp). |\n| Product: Category | Some product categories are sold below cost. | Ruled out | Every reliable segment is within 5 pp of 18.7%; worst is Shop By Sport at 19.6% (+0.9 pp). |\n| Time: quarterly trend | Losses have grown recently. | Ruled out | Every reliable period is within 5 pp of 18.7%; worst is 2016Q1 at 19.4% (+0.7 pp). |\n\n## Impact\n\n- No segment-level driver to size.\n- By total profit, Europe contributes the most ($1.17M, 29.5% of the total) and Africa the least ($252K, 6.3%).\n- By total sales, Europe contributes the most ($10.87M, 29.6% of the total) and Africa the least ($2.29M, 6.2%).\n\n## Recommendations\n\n| # | Action | Why | Impact | Effort | First step |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Set a price floor so no order line is sold below cost | No single segment explains the problem, so the fix has to be company-wide. | High | Medium | List the products and discount levels behind loss-making lines. |\n| 2 | Run a product-level profitability review | No single segment explains the problem, so the fix has to be company-wide. | High | Medium | Rank products by loss-making share and total profit. |\n| 3 | Track the loss-making share monthly by market and category | No single segment explains the problem, so the fix has to be company-wide. | Medium | Low | Automate this analysis as a monthly report. |\n\n## Method and caveats\n\n- Rule: a branch is a driver if its worst segment with at least 1,000 order lines is more than 5 pp worse than the baseline. Smaller segments are too noisy to act on.\n- Counts are order lines (a product within an order), not whole orders.\n- Periods with unusually low volume were left out of the trend test: 2017Q4, 2018Q1.\n- Produced by the scripted brain: fixed rules, no AI model. Every number comes from a tool result."
